ref 17592186045417] [17592186049697 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049697 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049697 #edb/ref 1031 "The Median of a Data Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049697 #edb/ref 1033 3749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049697 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0c1e857f-eb01-5d8f-abb2-6c7f92f87237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1031 "The Mode of a Data Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1033 3579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050034 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "27ba4add-eef0-5ff7-81cf-eae50262ceae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1033 2980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57fe5849-4b30-59b2-99bd-4fb2b776d659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188214 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188214 #edb/ref 1071 "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47507b72-86e2-5980-9e59-22d59c9f7279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188214 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} & =(12.5)^{2}+(11.7)^{2}+(13.2)^{2}+(12.8)^{2}+(12.1)^{2} \\\\  & =156.25+136.89+174.24+163.84+146.41 \\\\  & =777.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188215 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188215 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11f8e3f1-5110-5db1-83de-99d65ea76de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188215 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$ \n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Statistics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188279 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Statistics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188280 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1045 "Sampling Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188459 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3ed58cd9-6ef3-50be-9e26-519bae38fdf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1071 "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.34,\\,1.24,\\,1.42,\\,1.28,\\,1.39,\\,1.44\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4505d831-af23-5580-85dc-c9dcc976f63a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188550 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=1.34+1.24+1.42+1.28+1.39+1.44=8.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $8.11\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1061 37308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01b06219-bfde-5828-aaf4-a383feda218e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189085 #edb/ref 1061 37309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189085 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa1b7f8b-cb7d-5904-94ab-e048a827a448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189085 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189086 #edb/ref 1061 37318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9b58fb6-3fb7-5c00-add5-7c258fff9f89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189366 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that, given a random sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ of size $n,$ the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is an example of a **statistic**. A statistic is a random variable computed using values from a sample. Statistics are often used to estimate population parameters, although they also have other uses.\n\nLet's take a look at some other examples of statistics:\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population variance $\\sigma^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline{X})^2\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population maximum:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population range:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{max}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n) - \\textrm{min}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\n- The following statistic could be used as an estimator for the population median:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{median}(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)\n$$\n\nStatistics cannot include any *unknown* population parameters. For example:\n\n- If the population mean $\\mu$ is *unknown,* then the following quantity is *not* a statistic:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\n- However, if the population parameters $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are *both* known, then the following quantity *is* a statistic: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(\\dfrac{X_i - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, since statistics are described using observations from a random sample, they are random variables and therefore have a probability distribution. The probability distribution of a statistic is called the **sampling distribution** of the statistic." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189366 #edb/ref 1065 6146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189366 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff70a4bd-af92-57e6-b4c5-ea9a746555ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189366 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416864 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416869 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416869 #edb/ref 