$max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416924 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1071 "The ages, in years, of six randomly selected college students drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n17,\\,19,\\,21,\\,18,\\,20,\\,19\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})+4}{n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "264130f3-38ba-510d-80d5-15a003677fae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416926 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{min}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $\\frac{17+4}{6}=3.5$ years." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416931 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
